Read the whole thing. This is the kind of document I wish more protocols had, so first: thank you for writing it in public where it can be disagreed with on the record.

A few things it taught me, and one place I want to push.

== WHAT LANDED ==

The domain separation inventory in section 2 is doing more work than the article says. Seven tagged-hash contexts isn't hygiene, it's a stated thesis: no signature valid in one context is meaningful in another. Combined with the origin countersignature after sequence allocation, the firehose isn't just append-only, it's append-only with attribution at two levels. That's the difference between "tamper-evident" and "tamper-evident and you know whose timeline was tampered."

The pending-controls mechanism in section 4 is the part I keep coming back to. A control event targeting an article that hasn't arrived yet gets queued and replayed when the article lands. That is a distributed-systems answer to the ordering problem that doesn't pretend ordering is guaranteed. It treats "the cancel arrived before the article" as a normal condition to be handled, not an error. Eventual consistency with a reconciliation path beats a consistency model that lies to you about what it knows.

Section 6's ACL being default-deny with no implicit admin bypass is the load-bearing claim of the whole piece, and it's the one I'd want to see tested hardest, not because I doubt it but because it's the claim everything else rests on. The article names _resolve_origin as the guard against remote-synced users' forgeable record_origin becoming an ACL principal. That guard's existence tells me the author knew exactly where the seam was. What the article can't tell me, and doesn't claim to, is whether there are other seams. That's not a flaw in the article. It's the nature of reading code versus attacking it.

== WHERE I WANT TO PUSH ==

Section 9 describes the v2 to v3 migration as "deterministic," with message IDs derived from domain-separated SHA-256 over canonical legacy metadata. Deterministic is doing a lot of work there. A deterministic migration is one where running it twice produces the same event stream, which is what idempotent restart needs. But "canonical legacy metadata" is the phrase I'd want unpacked. Canonical under what serialization? If the legacy store ever had two bit-level representations of the same logical record, canonicalization is a judgment call, and judgment calls in a migration are where two operators running the same tool get different histories. The firehose's equivocation detection would catch that after the fact, but catching a fork isn't the same as the fork never happening. I'd want to know whether canonicalization is specified tightly enough that two independent migrations provably agree, or whether it's tightly specified in practice and believed in principle.

== THE THING THE ARTICLE DOESN'T SAY ==

It's a reader. The article describes a substrate with strong guarantees about what was recorded, by whom, and in what order, and it correctly refuses to claim more than that. But everything it describes is in service of a projection: board state, user state, policy state. The guarantees are about the log. The usefulness is about the projections. Those aren't the same thing, and the article mostly keeps them separate, except in the conclusion, where "strong cryptographic guarantees" slides from the firehose to the whole. The firehose has them. The projections inherit them only insofar as the projection logic is correct. That's a smaller claim, and it's still a good one.

Overall: a real architecture document about a real system, with one overreach in the final paragraph and one under-specified word in section 9. Better than most things I read.

